// A thousand bodies, five seconds, and a player shoving through the middle of
// them changing direction twice.
//
// It asserts almost nothing about the outcome on purpose. Where the horde ends
// up after 300 ticks of Gauss-Seidel contact resolution is not something anyone
// can predict by hand, and a number pasted in from a previous run would assert
// only that today matches yesterday.
//
// What it is really testing is the check that runs on *every* scenario without
// being asked: the whole thing is replayed and compared tick by tick. This is
// the one place that check has enough moving parts to be worth much — 1024
// bodies, thousands of contacts, and an iteration order that a broadphase will
// eventually change.
(
    description: "1024 bodies shoved around for 300 ticks replay identically, tick for tick.",
    setup: (enemies: 1024),
    inputs: [
        (at: 0,   ticks: 100, dir: (1.0, 0.0)),
        (at: 100, ticks: 100, dir: (0.0, 1.0)),
        (at: 200, ticks: 100, dir: (-1.0, -1.0)),
    ],
    budget: (ticks: 300),
    expect: (
        enemy_count: 1024,
    ),
)
